{
  "headline": "Syria condemns Houthi drone attack on Medina power station",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Syria’s Foreign Ministry condemned a Houthi drone attack on a power distribution station in Medina, Saudi Arabia, on Friday. According to the North Press Agency, the ministry stated the strike targeted civilian infrastructure, specifically a station that supplies electricity to the Prophet’s Mosque. The ministry further alleged that the attack violated Saudi Arabia’s sovereignty and threatened regional stability."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "GJA Vice-President urges journalists to drive constitutional reform in Ghana",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Rebecca Ekpe, the Vice-President of the Ghana Journalists Association (GJA), has called on journalists to lead efforts in ensuring that Ghana's constitutional reform is owned by its citizens. Speaking at a media engagement in Accra on October 1, 2026, organized by the Citizens Platform for the constitutional reforms, Ekpe stated that journalists should reflect on their constitutional responsibilities and ensure the revised Constitution represents the views of every Ghanaian rather than leaving the process to a select few. She noted that the media is an integral part of the conversation and that without it, implementation would face challenges. Ekpe called for inclusiveness, independence, integrity, and excellence in the process, stating that journalists must scrutinize the exercise of public power to ensure every voice is heard. The Citizens Platform consists of more than 100 Civil Society Organisations (CSOs). Adem Abebe, a Senior Advisor at International IDEA, added that the success of the reforms depends on the media and that its role should be protected, describing the media as a powerful avenue for shaping opinions. The media training session was designed to guide practitioners through various position papers provided by CSOs, the Institute of Economic Affairs, labour unions, and the New Patriotic Party (NPP). The training aimed to seek the views of media practitioners on synchronizing these position papers and examining how they might affect existing legislation."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "California Governor claims Iran war fallout is accelerating clean energy investment",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "California Governor Gavin Newsom has described U.S. President Donald Trump as a “gift” to the environmental movement due to the consequences of the war involving the U.S., Israel, and Iran. Speaking to The Guardian during Climate Week in New York, Newsom argued that the conflict has exposed vulnerabilities regarding energy security and disruptions in the Middle East, which he claims is accelerating investments in renewables and clean energy. Newsom, a potential contender for the Democratic presidential nomination, characterized the president as an “unintentional biophile.”"
    },
    {
      "text": "Since the Iran war began in February, the strait of Hormuz—which typically handles one-fifth of the world’s oil and gas exports—has been routinely snarled, though it is now starting to open up. This disruption has caused global fossil fuel costs to soar. According to Carbon Brief, global fossil fuel emissions are estimated to drop by 0.5% this year due to the crisis. Additionally, the International Energy Agency stated that the peak in oil consumption may arrive this year rather than the previously estimated 2029. While some countries have turned to solar and wind energy, there has also been a small rise in coal use. Separately, the president's imposition of tariffs and the importation of foreign ground beef may impact the U.S. cattle industry, which is a source of greenhouse gas emissions."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Olivia Chow pledges free family programming if re-elected in Toronto",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Toronto mayoral candidate Olivia Chow has promised to implement free weekend programming for children at public libraries if re-elected. According to CBC Toronto, Chow also proposed expanding the Toronto Public Library program known as tpl:map by 1,000 new passes, which provides free family admission to local museums and attractions. The proposal was discussed as mayoral candidates returned to the campaign trail on Saturday."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Stakeholder calls for National Political History and Heritage Centre in Saltpond, Ghana",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Ernest De-Graft Egyir, President of the Mfantseman-Saltpond Development Alliance (MSDA), has called for the establishment of a National Political History and Heritage Centre in Saltpond, Ghana. Speaking at a Founders’ Day commemoration themed “Saltpond Heritage, Ghana’s Future, Turning History into Opportunity,” Egyir proposed that the center be used to preserve the country's political history and transform local historic sites into opportunities for tourism, research, education, and economic development. He suggested the institution should document Ghana's transition from colonial rule to independence by preserving political archives, oral histories, photographs, and other records. Egyir stated the initiative should be national rather than partisan, aiming to tell Ghana's story comprehensively and with respect for the various movements that contributed to independence. He further proposed linking the center to a wider heritage corridor to connect historical sites with hospitality, local enterprise, and tourism. As part of the proposed development, Egyir cited Canaan Lodge, the site where the United Gold Coast Convention (UGCC) was launched on August 4, 1947, as a key landmark. Egyir called on the government, the Ministry of Tourism, Culture and Creative Arts, the Ghana Tourism Authority, traditional authorities, universities, historians, the private sector, development partners, and the diaspora to support the initiative."
    }
  ]
}

