  "headline": "Cockroach Janta Party announces Delhi march and calls for election chief's resignation",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The Cockroach Janta Party has announced a march in Delhi scheduled for October 10. The party has called for the resignation of Chief Election Commissioner Gyanesh Kumar, stating a protest will occur if he does not resign."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Ghanaian Minister calls for translating CSIR research into commercial products",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Dr. Zaneto Agyeman-Rawlings, Ghana's Minister of Environment, Science, Technology and Innovation, has called for increased collaboration between the Council for Scientific and Industrial Research (CSIR), the government, and the private sector to convert research into commercially viable products for national development. During a working visit to the CSIR in Accra as part of a familiarization tour of agencies under her ministry, the Minister stated there is a need to bridge the gap between scientific research and the public to ensure Ghanaians and the private sector can access and benefit from innovations developed by local scientists and researchers. Dr. Agyeman-Rawlings noted that a disconnect between research, academia, and the public can make it difficult for the private sector to utilize CSIR work, despite the institution's expertise across various sectors."
    },
    {
      "text": "To address this, the Minister urged the CSIR to collaborate with the Ministry’s Public Relations Directorate to showcase research and innovations through communication platforms. The Ministry intends to display samples of products developed by CSIR institutes—including cereals, agricultural products, and other innovations—at its reception. Additionally, the Ministry plans to use its website, social media platforms, screens, and display panels to highlight the work of researchers. Dr. Agyeman-Rawlings stated these initiatives aim to make science more relatable to young people and encourage them to view science as a career field with practical applications."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Somali President says country will not accept Israeli presence 'under any circumstances'",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "In an exclusive interview with Al Jazeera, Somali President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud stated that Somalia will not accept an Israeli presence in the country “under any circumstances” and will resist attempts to be pulled into Middle East crises. Mohamud described Israel as a “destabilising force” that does not abide by international law and criticized the country's recognition of Somaliland in December. He further alleged that Israel is planning to establish a naval base in Berbera and utilize the city's airport, suggesting the aim is to gain proximity to Iran and the Gulf states. Berbera is a port city on the Gulf of Aden coast in Somaliland, a self-declared republic in northwestern Somalia that is claimed by Mogadishu. The city faces Yemen near the Bab al-Mandeb strait, an area where Iran-aligned Houthis have previously enforced blockades on Israel-linked ships."
    },
    {
      "text": "Mohamud characterized Israeli plans as part of a “Greater Israel” project, a term referring to various proposals for Israeli control over territory beyond its current borders. While Israel has not adopted this as official policy, Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu stated in an August 2025 interview that he felt connected to the vision, a comment that drew condemnation from Arab governments. Mohamud stated, “We will overcome the challenges posed to our country by Israeli moves.”"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Durham Region organization adopts grocery-style food bank model to address rising demand",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Feed the Need Durham reports that food bank visits in the Durham Region of Canada have surged since the pandemic. To address this demand, the organization is transitioning to a grocery-style model. According to the organization, this model is intended to provide people with more choice and help make better use of food supplies."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "US candidate endorsed by Nancy Pelosi calls for full arms embargo on Israel",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Connie Chan, a San Francisco supervisor and US Democrat running to succeed former House of Representatives Speaker Nancy Pelosi in California’s 11th congressional district, has called for a “full arms embargo on the Israeli government.” During a televised debate on October 1, 2026, Chan stated that “on day one in Congress” she would vote to “end a genocide in Gaza and to end an apartheid state.” Chan also told debate moderators she would not support funding for Israel’s Iron Dome missile defense system and argued that humanitarian aid must be prioritized. Chan is endorsed by Pelosi, who is not seeking re-election and who historically supported Israel by voting for military aid and opposing sanctions or boycotts. Chan is facing fellow Democrat and California state Senator Scott Wiener in the race to succeed Pelosi. Wiener has also described Israel’s assault on Gaza as a genocide and stated he would oppose the provision of bombs, offensive weapons, or funding for the Israeli military. Wiener characterized the Israeli government as an “absolute abomination” regarding its actions in Gaza and its empowerment of settler violence and land grabs in the West Bank. The contest occurs ahead of November midterm elections, in which all 435 House seats and 35 Senate seats are at stake. The race follows recent divisions in Congress regarding US support for Israel, including an unsuccessful effort by nearly all Senate Democrats to require a State Department report on Israel’s human rights practices."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "NPP delegates approve 14 constitutional amendments in Kumasi, Ghana",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Delegates of the New Patriotic Party (NPP) have approved 14 amendments to the party's constitution during a National Delegates Conference in Kumasi, Ghana. The amendments, which were proposed by the party’s Constitutional Amendments Committee, were adopted through a series of voice votes as part of proceedings that also include the election of national executives. According to MyJoyOnline, the changes aim to strengthen internal structures, administration, and electoral preparedness. 14 specific amendments were approved to address various aspects of internal organization, leadership, and administration."
    },
    {
      "text": "The approved amendments include several structural changes to party committees and leadership roles. To increase representation on the National Disciplinary Committee, Articles 4(17)(c) and 16(9) were amended to increase the representation of the National Council of Elders from one to two members. At the constituency level, Article 7(2) was amended to provide for the appointment of an additional constituency vice chairperson to the Constituency Executive Committee. 14 amendments also include the inclusion of Regional and Constituency Information Technology Officers as members of their respective executive committees through amendments to Articles 7(2) and 9(1). Additionally, the amendments provide for Deputy Regional Communications Officers and Deputy Constituency Communications Officers to serve as members of their respective executive committees, following the transition of primary communications officers from appointed to elected positions."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Syrian Muslim Brotherhood holds second Shura Council meeting since fall of Assad regime",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The Syrian Muslim Brotherhood announced on September 27, 2026, that it held a Shura Council meeting, marking the second such meeting since the fall of the Assad regime. According to Syria Direct, the group is currently weighing its political future amid internal divisions and regional pressure as Syria undergoes a transition that leaves the status of political parties uncertain. In a statement, the group applauded the convening of the Syrian parliament and emphasized its importance for establishing organized political pluralism. Abdulrahman Alhaj, a specialist on Islamist groups, noted that the group's recent statement regarding partnering with national forces to rebuild the country indicates a preparation to participate in elections. However, as of October 2, 2026, the group faces external pressure to disband and continues to navigate internal challenges. The legal standing of political parties in Syria remains unsettled; while a 2025 announcement by al-Sharaa dissolved the Baath Party and parties affiliated with the National Progressive Front, a subsequent Constitutional Declaration stated that political participation and party formation will be governed by a new law that has not yet been drafted. Additionally, newly adopted procedures for parliament's work will ban political blocs and parties within the legislative body."
    },
    {
      "text": "Founded in 1945 as an offshoot of the Egyptian Islamist movement, the Syrian Muslim Brotherhood was banned in 1980 by President Hafez al-Assad in response to growing Islamist militancy, which made membership in the group a capital offense. Following an attempted armed rebellion by an offshoot of the organization in 1982, the Syrian regime responded with the Hama massacre, resulting in the arrest or death of tens of thousands of civilians and Brotherhood supporters and driving the group into exile."
    }
  ]
}
